import json
from pathlib import Path
p=Path(__file__).parent;r=json.loads((p/'reviews.json').read_text())
base='https://www.olimpiadas.spm.pt/Downloads/Ficheiros/Provas/XLIII%20OPM(2024-25)/XLIII_f'
rows=[(1,2.5,'Number theory',['linear recurrences','divisibility'],'Express the sixth term in the second and third, then solve a bounded linear Diophantine equation.',None),(2,4,'Geometry',['auxiliary points','equilateral triangles'],'An auxiliary circumcentre or reflection creates an equilateral triangle and a rhombus, making the angle chase work.',r'Convex quadrilateral $ABCD$ has acute angles at $A,D$, and $AB=BC=CD$. If $\angle BDA=30^\circ$, prove $\angle DAC=30^\circ$.'),(3,5,'Combinatorics',['dynamics','monotonicity','fixed points'],'After the first iteration every entry is nondecreasing; a fixed-point count gives the sharp triangular-number threshold.',r'Replace each entry of a list by the number of times its value occurs in that list, doing all replacements simultaneously. Starting with any list of 2025 positive integers, repeat. Find the largest integer $k$ for which some entry must eventually be at least $k$.'),(4,2,'Geometry',['coordinates','rhombus area'],'The perpendicular diagonals locate the second diagonal; direct area calculation gives three.' ,None),(5,5.5,'Number theory',['divisor sets','difference sets'],'Normalize the minimum to zero; the largest proper divisor and complementary differences sharply restrict the possible set.',r'Find every integer $n\ge2$ for which there is a finite set of integers whose positive pairwise differences are exactly the positive proper divisors of $n$.'),(6,6.5,'Combinatorics',['directed graphs','degree counting'],'In the finite state graph, equal incoming and outgoing counts would force backward closure; a shrinking-pile predecessor contradicts that closure.',r'Distinct cards form two ordered piles. For each pair of cards, a fixed rule specifies which wins. On each move take both top cards and put them, in either chosen order, at the bottom of the winner’s pile. Prove that suitable choices always lead to all cards lying in one pile, for every starting deal and every rule table.')]
for n,v,topic,tags,reason,statement in rows:
 id='prt_2025_finalb_'+str(n);day=1 if n<=3 else 2;src=base+str(day)+'b.pdf';sol=base+str(day)+'bs.pdf';(p/(id+'.json')).write_text(json.dumps({'country':'Portugal','competition':'Portuguese Mathematical Olympiad','year':2025,'section':'National Final — Category B, Day '+str(day),'problem_number':str(n),'problem_markdown':statement or '', 'solutions_markdown':[],'booklet_title':'XLIII OPM — Final Nacional, Categoria B'},indent=2))
 r[id]={'difficulty':v,'topic':topic,'tags':tags,'reason':reason,'sourceUrl':src,'solutionUrl':sol,'sourceName':'Sociedade Portuguesa de Matemática — official final paper and solutions','solutionKind':'source-link','solution':'[Official worked solution (Portuguese)]('+sol+').','license':'Original competition rights retained; concise English mathematical paraphrase.'}
(p/'reviews.json').write_text(json.dumps(r,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2))
